        <mj-text
          padding="16px 12px 10px 12px"
          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="white"
        >
          <h2
            style="
              padding-bottom: 8px;
              color: #06266d;
              text-align: left;
              border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d;
              font-size: 26px;
              line-height: 1.2;
              font-weight: 300;
            "
          >
            Analysis
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          padding="10px 12px"
          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="#000000"
        >
          <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
  US intervention legacy
</h2>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Summary: US strikes highlight America’s long history of regime-changing interventions abroad.</strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>What happened? </strong>President Trump initially framed the US-Israeli strikes on Iran as eliminating Tehran’s nuclear and military threat and potentially removing the ruling clerical regime. However, officials, including Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth, later denied that the conflict is intended as a regime-change war. The debate reflects a long <a href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-checkered-history-of-us-regime-change-operations/a-76200309" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">US history of interventions</a> aimed at reshaping governments abroad.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>US interventions: </strong>US interventions abroad have taken many forms, including covert operations, military invasions, and support for opposition groups to influence political outcomes. During the Cold War, the US intervened to counter communism, including the 1953 coup in Iran and the 1965 intervention in the Dominican Republic. Later operations included invasions of Grenada (1983) and Panama (1989). In the 21st century, major interventions occurred in Afghanistan in 2001, Iraq in 2003, and Libya in 2011.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Outcomes:</strong> US interventions abroad have produced mixed results. In some cases, they successfully removed targeted leaders or regimes and reshaped political systems. However, many interventions created power vacuums, weakened institutions, and prolonged instability. Over time, these operations have also shaped global perceptions of US foreign policy, fueling debates about sovereignty, legitimacy, and the effectiveness of military-led political change.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
