        <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
    What is Juche philosophy?
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border="1px solid #00000013"
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>

<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Ideology:</strong> Juche is <a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/Juche" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">North Korea’s official state ideology</a>, created by Kim Il Sung and later expanded by his son Kim Jong Il. Juche is often described as “self-reliance,” meaning a nation should rely on its own strength and shape its own future. It calls for independence in all aspects, including political, economic, and ideological. It is core to socialist construction.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Principle:</strong> Juche holds that people, not material conditions, are the main force in revolution and state-building. It rejects materialist determinism (the idea that human actions are fully shaped by economic or material conditions) and argues that political will can surpass objective constraints. It also prioritizes national sovereignty.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Roots:</strong> Juche emerged from Marxism-Leninism. North Korea’s ruling Workers’ Party began within the communist movement, but its leaders argued that Korea needed a model suited to its own history. North Korean theorists say Juche develops Marxism to a “higher level” by emphasizing national independence and rejecting strict Soviet-style socialism. Kim Jong Il described Juche as a creative form of Marxism adapted to Korea’s experience.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><em>See a brief video about the ideology </em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mH9zmcURlkc" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;"><strong><em>here</em></strong></a><em>.</em></p>
</mj-text>
